  "worked_solution": "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/88b5fde383f094436dd94adefa7912f1a194319739fdb0b8a6a9a74693362219.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
